#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199257} :activity/id #uuid "be4ab3e9-bce0-5963-aee7-f144ec42c0bc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049110 :topic/id #uuid "db34e5aa-cabd-518f-9dff-177947a5ce3a" :topic/math-academy-id 4166} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199256 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199454 :tutorial/content "Recall that a lamina's *center of mass* is the point $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})$ where its total mass could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the coordinate axes as the original lamina. \n\nThe center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left( \\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\\:$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nRemember that if we could place a fulcrum under our lamina at its center of mass, it would balance perfectly." :tutorial/id #uuid "dbc92201-9cb2-54d6-9ac9-8ee49bf8c4e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4905 :tutorial/title "Center of Mass"} :step/id #uuid "274308ee-bab4-54df-9e77-953f260e7982" :step/math-academy-id 30624 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199258}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199257 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199455 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the center of mass of a thin lamina whose mass is unevenly distributed. \n\nWe describe the mass distribution across a lamina using its mass density function $\\lambda (x,y).$ This function tells us the *mass per unit area* at any point $(x,y)$ on the lamina.\n\nFor example, consider a thin plate with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = 6xy$ occupying the finite rectangular region $D$ between the lines $x=1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=1.$ What is the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis?\n\nTo answer this, consider a point $(x,y) \\in D$ on the plate and a small rectangular piece with its bottom-left corner at $(x,y)$ and area $\\Delta{A},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8cd947e542aaf7eb787abc7a846e87a2d03ccafa0987f3422928fdcba115da9e.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{A}$ is very small, we can assume that our density function is approximately constant over the selected piece. As a result, the moment of this piece about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{M}_{y} & ≈x\\cdot Δm \\\\  & ≈x\\cdot 6xy\\,ΔA \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y\\,ΔA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total moment of the plate about the axis, we add together the moments of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta{A} \\to 0.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\nM_y =  \\iint\\limits_D 6x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the integration, we find that the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(8-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe moment about the $x$-axis can be found similarly.\n\nIn general, the moments $M_x$ and $M_y$ of a lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $x$-axis and about the $y$-axis, respectively, are given by the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ {M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7aa18cfe-acd7-5e17-982c-58eabc063404" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4906 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "751210ed-66b0-57b2-8130-be9ebe7e72a9" :step/math-academy-id 30625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199330}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199258 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198364 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198238 :question/id #uuid "8199670b-efe4-537b-8f8a-228d567a0944" :question/math-academy-id "e-8182"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f7a06a8-0260-5b59-b3a5-c02ee03980d4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419237}] :knowledge-point/title "Find the Center of Mass of a Plane Lamina"} :step/id #uuid "d8381e14-c44b-5f31-9e2d-03ef40423eb9" :step/math-academy-id 30626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199259}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199259 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199456 :tutorial/content "When a lamina's mass density is constant, the center of mass depends only on the shape of the lamina. In cases like these, the center of mass is called the **centroid** of the lamina. The centroid of a shape corresponds to its geometrical center.\n\nSo, $\\lambda$ is constant, then the mass of the plate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda \\,dA \\\\  & =\\lambda {∬}_{D}\\,dA \\\\  & =\\lambda \\cdot {A}_{D},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A_D$ denotes the area of $D.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nm = \\lambda \\cdot A_D \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\dfrac{\\lambda}{m} = \\dfrac{1}{A_D}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the formula for the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m} & =\\frac{1}{m}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda \\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{\\lambda }{m}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can similarly compute the $y$-coordinate.\n\nIn general, the centroid of a lamina $D$ with area $A_D$ is given by $(x_c, y_c),$ where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A, \\qquad \n\\displaystyle y_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "aea964ff-901c-5b76-9fca-721d53f04354" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4907 :tutorial/title "Centroids"} :step/id #uuid "c6144c3d-16c1-5ac5-841d-01a96fe71c2e" :step/math-academy-id 30627 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199260}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198365 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198239 :question/id #uuid "37d7f2ae-90b0-5e6f-83c2-7cb21ed4f089" :question/math-academy-id "e-8183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "255c9985-5d85-5fd5-a2b9-c4752f04afac" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419239}] :knowledge-point/title "Find the Centroid of a Plane Lamina"} :step/id #uuid "38ffe571-8650-5b48-a6db-d2d7374b7548" :step/math-academy-id 30628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198406 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198048 :question/id #uuid "81b0b6e1-41c3-5329-806e-162b8b27b045" :question/math-academy-id "e-12466"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "804ee2d1-5590-534d-ae11-7d2c5614a9b1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419240}] :knowledge-point/title "The Moment of a Plane Lamina About the Y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "c6197786-2f72-519b-bd74-410c8f09a532" :step/math-academy-id 44879 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199256}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198407 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198049 :question/id #uuid "9294903c-1b4c-58b6-a878-7f2220ec763d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12470"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "444e9b54-f6d4-5b70-af49-d01556bb3135" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050409}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419241}] :knowledge-point/title "The Moment of a Plane Lamina About the X-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "6a2f3763-0f5f-5328-a240-5a9328c979b3" :step/math-academy-id 44905 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199329}}] :activity/title "Moments and Centers of Mass of Plane Laminas" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198495}]}
